**Part 2**

The merger of black holes as two objects of different masses should proceed according to the following scheme:

Firstly, due to their enormous mutual gravity, there can be no question of physical contact, even of their event horizons. Their gravitational interaction precedes and begins long before their approach. The event horizon is already their outer boundary, where the speed of infalling matter approaches the speed of light.

Secondly, based on the proposed model, the merger is an act of stripping the shell from the less massive (weaker) object. This occurs as a tidal interaction between two bodies with high intrinsic gravity.

When two black holes of different masses approach, the more massive hole tears the shell of the satellite hole at a distance, exposing the region inside the black hole that contains no space or time. This is analogous to the filling of a cavitation bubble by the external medium. A hydraulic shock of space against itself occurs, and a gravitational wave is formed, which is not particularly related to the mass of the absorbed black hole. The black hole acting as the absorber, having received additional mass, is forced, as a dynamic gyroscope, to adjust its rotation parameters. It can do this using only two parameters: rotation speed and diameter, and according to only one criterion — the impossibility of the equatorial rotation speed exceeding the fundamental speed of light.

In this case, ground-based instruments record a rising noise (accretion), then a hydraulic shock (disappearance of the weaker black hole), and a decaying noise (vibration of the diameter of the resulting black hole as it adjusts its rotation).

Thus, we have a gravitational wave event profile in the form of "noise — peak — noise." The concluding noise in the terminology of modern physics is called ringdown.

How does the fatal disintegration of a black hole differ from the merger of two black holes?

It occurs in two phases. In the case of a merger, there is a pronounced single peak on the merger signature (one object disappears, the other adjusts). In the case of final self-destruction, for example, in a "black hole — neutron star" pair, the following events occur sequentially:

- The absorption by the black hole of a mass comparable to its own causes the appearance of an accretion disk, which destabilizes the shape of the black hole and leads to its equatorial disintegration. The external space gains access to the region devoid of space, and a hydraulic shock of space (cavitation) occurs. This produces the first bright peak on the signature.
- Then, the remnants of the black hole, in the form of two hemispheres, which continue to rotate and experience mutual attraction, collapse in a flat explosion and form a characteristic decay pattern. This was recorded by the Hubble telescope in the example of object V445 Puppis. These are symmetrical "teacups" connected at their bases.

The collapse of the flattened hemispheres produces the second peak on the gravitational wave profile of the decay event. The flat equatorial disintegration of a black hole directly explains the flat shape of spiral galaxies.

That is all, but the question of the merger of two black holes of similar mass remains open. It is possible that it will provide an answer to the origin of globular star clusters, when the fatal merger of two black holes could lead to a spherical explosion without the leading role of an accretion disk.

