**Part 3**

The question of the properties of space and its characteristics is a question of visible interaction. It is a question of analogies that describe the logic of nature. Nature does not favor randomness but prefers systemic structures: similarity, dualism, fractals.

Consider such a fundamental type of fractal as the binary number series. Its structure is defined by two entities — zero and one. Zero is an incompressible symbol formatting the numerical space, and one is a parameter of its curvature. Using zeros and ones, according to the simplest rule of inversion, the entire fractal is constructed, which is fundamentally based on powers of the base "2" and intermediate values. Powers of the base "2" are always 1 with N zeros. This is the discreteness of similarity. But the main point is that without incompressible zeros, this fractal is impossible. Here, mathematics indirectly hints that all types of spaces possess certain properties. Any wave, for its existence, requires a medium that can realize the wave function based on its physical properties. The properties of waves directly depend on the properties of the medium. The speed of sound depends on the density of the medium; the speed of light in glass differs from its speed in a vacuum. The motion of a conductor in a magnetic field induces induction, which limits the growth of its speed through a counter-effect.

By the way, if we theoretically (!) assume that a certain particle could be accelerated to a speed exceeding the speed of light, what could happen?
Two paradoxical things would occur, indicating that "The task is impossible!"

Firstly, an unsolvable paradox of delivering energy to the accelerated particle arises. The speed of energy delivery for acceleration would also have to exceed the speed of light, and it is impossible to supply the particle with an infinite amount of energy before the start of acceleration.

Secondly, Lorentz contraction in this case becomes imaginary (the square root of a negative number in the denominator of the equation), which also indicates the domain of inapplicability of the equation. Mathematical equations have no other way to protest against their application beyond their domain of applicability except to produce an infinite or imaginary result. But this is also a result, not a breakdown of the formula. The formula reveals the extrapolation beyond its applicability. Not all extrapolations predict the true pattern. A segment of a sinusoid on an ascending portion cannot predict its cyclicity.

